from pathlib import Path
r=Path.cwd();out=r/'work/reference-physics-publish/docs'
s=(r/'docs/flow-journey.html').read_text().replace('72,000','108,000').replace('Two complementary W11 projections.','Two complementary W11 projections plus a separate generic-wing experiment.')
s=s.replace('<button id="pause">','<label class="check"><input id="pressure-arrows" type="checkbox">Pressure-force arrows</label><button id="pause">')
s=s.replace('<p class="legend">','<div class="presets"><button data-preset="speed">Dense flow</button><button data-preset="vorticity">Wake rotation</button><button data-preset="pressure">Pressure + force arrows</button></div><p class="legend">')
s=s.replace('&amp;v=journey1','&amp;v=refs1')
section='''<section class="panel"><h2>Wing-angle experiment <small>generic NACA 2412 · NOT a W11 wing · separate chord-based Re = 1,000</small></h2>
<div class="controls"><label>Angle of attack <output id="angle-out">4°</output><input id="wing-angle" type="range" min="0" max="24" step="1" value="4"></label><label class="check"><input id="wing-inverted" type="checkbox">Invert wing camber</label><p>Changing angle restarts the flow at a fixed incidence. Observe separation / reverse flow and vorticity as the field develops. No stall angle or lift coefficient is measured; do not apply aircraft stall thresholds to this low-Re grid.</p></div>
<iframe id="wing" title="Generic NACA 2412 wing-angle and separation learning experiment, not W11 geometry" src="side-flow.html?embed=1&amp;journey=1&amp;wing=1&amp;v=refs1"></iframe></section>
<section class="panel calculations"><h2>Movable-wing trade-off <small>equation-based hypothetical examples — NOT W11 results or fluid-solver input</small></h2>
<div class="controls"><label>Example road speed (km/h)<input id="example-speed" type="range" min="60" max="350" value="300"><output id="example-speed-out">300 km/h · 83.33 m/s</output></label><label>Assumed reference area (m²)<input id="example-area" type="number" min="0.1" max="10" step="0.1" value="1.5"></label></div>
<p>F↓ = ½ρv²SC↓ &nbsp; · &nbsp; D = ½ρv²SCᴅ &nbsp; · &nbsp; ρ = 1.225 kg/m³. Positive C↓ denotes downforce here. The estimated coefficients below come from the third reference and are not calibrated to this car.</p><div id="tradeoff" aria-live="polite"></div><p>Speed changes here update equations only, not the W11 calculation or wing geometry. The W11 is a 2020 reference model; this is not a claim that it had the later active front/rear-wing system.</p></section>
<section class="panel references"><h2>What the three references contributed</h2><div class="reference-cards">
<article><h3>1 · Separation and stall</h3><p>A wing-angle experiment lets you inspect computed vorticity and reverse flow. Thin-airfoil lift trends only describe attached flow; they do not predict stall. No fixed stall threshold is imposed.</p><a href="https://x.com/matharium/status/2124201568899883036">Matharium · wing stall</a></article>
<article><h3>2 · Pressure and wakes</h3><p>Click fluid cells to compare solver pressure with the ideal Bernoulli speed relation. Toggle qualitative pressure-force arrows. Wake vortices come from the solver, not manually added vortex objects. The ideal relation need not match a viscous, unsteady wake.</p><a href="https://x.com/themathpulse/status/2123494395274240216">Math Pulse · bodies, pressure and wakes</a></article>
<article><h3>3 · Downforce versus drag</h3><p>The calculator compares two assumed coefficient sets and illustrates squared-speed scaling. These illustrative forces are separate from CFD and are not measured W11 loads.</p><a href="https://x.com/matharium/status/2124982069285785662">Matharium · movable-wing trade-off</a></article></div><p>Reference captions were inspected; the X videos could not be played. This is an original implementation of the described concepts, not an exact recreation of unseen footage or copied source code.</p><p>Physics background: <a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/lift-equation/">NASA lift equation</a> · <a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/boundary-layer/">NASA boundary layers and separation</a>.</p></section>
'''
s=s.replace('<footer>',section+'<footer>').replace('flow-journey.js','flow-journey.js?v=20261124-refs1').replace('flow-journey.css','flow-journey.css?v=20261124-refs1')
(out/'flow-journey.html').write_text(s)
s=(r/'docs/flow-journey.css').read_text()+'''\n.presets{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}.check{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px}.check input{width:auto}.reference-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:24px;padding:20px}.references>p,.calculations>p{padding:0 20px;font-size:12px;color:#c2b5ce}.reference-cards article{font-size:12px;color:#c2b5ce}.reference-cards h3{color:#ffdb98}.controls p{font-size:11px;flex-basis:100%;margin:0;color:#baabc7}input[type=number]{padding:8px;background:#100e18;border:1px solid #ffffff40;color:white}#tradeoff{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px;padding:20px}#tradeoff article{background:#27212f;padding:16px;border-radius:8px}#tradeoff strong{color:#ffdb98;font-size:24px}#tradeoff span{display:block;font-size:12px}@media(max-width:700px){.reference-cards,#tradeoff{grid-template-columns:1fr}}\n'''
